window.MOD={"n": 1, "title": "The Language of Movement", "hours": 1.5, "tagline": "A shared vocabulary of planes, axes and joint movements lets you cue any pose clearly and understand every module that follows.", "overview": "Before you can explain why a pose works, you need words that mean the same thing to everyone in the room. This module builds that vocabulary: anatomical position, directional terms, the three planes of the body with their axes, and the paired terms for joint movement, from flexion and extension to the special words for the ankle, forearm and shoulder blade.\n\nWe learn it all through three standing poses you already know: Mountain, Warrior II and Triangle. You will also meet the six tissues that every pose loads, with a clear line between what research supports and what remains yoga lore. Throughout, the rule for teachers is simple: adapt and refer, never diagnose.", "objectives": ["Describe anatomical position and explain how it differs from Mountain pose", "Identify the sagittal, frontal and transverse planes and the axis of each in Mountain, Warrior II and Triangle", "Name the joint movements of a standing pose, joint by joint, using correct paired terms", "Define the special movement terms for the ankle, foot, forearm and shoulder blade and give a pose example of each", "Compare bone, cartilage, ligament, tendon, fascia and muscle by function and ability to adapt", "Distinguish well supported anatomy claims from common yoga myths"], "lessons": [{"id": "1.1", "title": "Why anatomy makes a better yoga teacher", "mins": 15, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Anatomy is a teaching tool, not a trivia contest", "points": ["Clear anatomy words make your cues precise and repeatable", "Knowing structure helps you adapt poses to real bodies", "It helps you spot when a student needs a referral", "It keeps your claims honest and evidence based"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": [], "caption": "Even Mountain pose is full of anatomy worth naming"}, "notes": "Welcome to Anatomy on the Mat. I have taught anatomy and physiology since 2007, and I am also a chiropractor, so I spend a lot of time with bodies that do not match the textbook picture. That is exactly why this course exists. You do not need to memorise every muscle to teach well. You need a shared vocabulary so your cues mean the same thing every time, enough structure to understand why a pose feels different in different bodies, and the judgement to know when something is outside your lane. Anatomy gives you all three. Think of it as a tool that sits in your teaching kit next to breath, sequencing and voice."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "4", "label": "steps in every lesson", "body": "See it, Feel it, Cue it, Check it. You learn the structure, find it in your own body, turn it into words for class, then test yourself.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "tree", "highlight": ["footL", "shinL"], "joints": ["ankleL"], "caption": "Feel it: your standing ankle is busy in Tree"}, "notes": "Every lesson in this course follows the same four steps. See it means we look at the anatomy, usually with a diagram or a pose figure. Feel it means you find that structure or movement in your own body on the mat, because anatomy you have felt sticks far better than anatomy you have only read. Cue it means we turn the idea into plain words you could say to a room of beginners. Check it means a quick question or two so you know it landed. Take Tree as an example: you can see the ankle joint on a diagram, feel the tiny corrections of your standing ankle, and cue students to let those wobbles happen."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Yoga lore and anatomy: what holds up", "columns": [{"head": "Well supported", "points": ["Yoga can improve strength, balance and mobility", "Slow breathing can calm many people", "Regular loading makes tissues adapt"]}, {"head": "Not supported", "points": ["Twists wring toxins out of organs", "Stretching releases stored emotions from fascia", "One perfect alignment fits every body"]}], "notes": "You will hear many claims in yoga spaces. Some hold up well: regular practice can improve strength, balance and range of motion, and slow breathing reliably helps many people feel calmer. Other claims are not supported. Twists do not squeeze toxins out of your liver; your liver and kidneys handle that job continuously. Emotions are not stored in fascia in any way science has shown. And there is no single perfect alignment that suits every skeleton, because bones vary in shape. Throughout this course I will be plain about what is established and what is not. That honesty protects your students and your credibility."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: notice before you name", "steps": ["Stand in Mountain with feet hip width and arms relaxed by your sides", "Sway slowly forward and back; notice where pressure travels under your feet", "Sway side to side; notice which muscles switch on at hips and ankles", "Come to stillness and list three things you felt, in plain words"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["footL", "footR"], "joints": ["ankleL", "ankleR"], "caption": "Small sways reveal the ankle and hip strategies of balance"}, "notes": "Before we learn any new words, I want you to notice. Stand in Mountain and let yourself sway very slowly forward and back, like a tree in a light breeze. You will feel pressure move toward your toes and then your heels, and you will probably feel your calves and shins working. Now sway side to side and notice the hips joining in. Come back to stillness and name three things you felt in your own words. Later in this module you will learn the proper terms for these movements. The point is that feeling comes first and vocabulary makes it shareable."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Adapt and refer, never diagnose", "cue": "Let's find a version of this pose that feels steady for you today, and if something hurts, come out and tell me after class.", "why": "Pain has many causes that a yoga teacher cannot assess. Your job is to offer options and suggest a qualified professional when pain persists.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "child", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": [], "caption": "Child's pose is always a valid option"}, "notes": "This is the most important boundary in the whole course. Anatomy knowledge can tempt teachers to play clinician: you tore your meniscus, your pelvis is out, that is sciatica. Please never do this. Even trained clinicians need a history, an examination and sometimes imaging to make a diagnosis. What you can do is adapt the pose, offer a rest option such as Child's pose, and encourage a student with persistent or sharp pain, numbness or tingling to see a doctor or physical therapist. That is not a weakness in your teaching. It is exactly what a skilled, safe teacher does."}, {"type": "check", "question": "A student says her knee hurts in Warrior II. What is the best first response from a yoga teacher?", "options": ["Offer a modified version or rest, and suggest she see a clinician if pain persists", "Tell her she probably has a torn meniscus", "Ask her to push through so the knee gets stronger"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Teachers adapt and refer; they do not diagnose or encourage working through pain.", "notes": "Take a moment with this one. The first option reflects the scope we just discussed: modify, rest, and refer if the pain persists. Naming a diagnosis is outside a yoga teacher's scope and could be wrong. Pushing through pain is never good advice in a group class."}], "guide": {"brief": "Anatomy makes you a better yoga teacher for three practical reasons. First, it gives you precise language. When you say lift your arms in the frontal plane or bend your front knee, every student hears the same instruction. Second, it helps you understand variation. Bones, joints and connective tissue differ from person to person, so the same pose will look and feel different in different bodies. Knowing why lets you offer options instead of forcing one shape. Third, it helps you recognise when something is beyond a yoga class, so you can refer a student to a qualified clinician.\n\nThis course uses a four step method in every lesson. See it: look at the structure. Feel it: find it in your own body on the mat. Cue it: turn it into plain words for class. Check it: answer a few questions to confirm you understand. The method mirrors how adults learn movement best, by linking a concept to a physical sensation and then using it.\n\nYou will also learn to separate evidence from lore. Many popular claims, such as twists detoxifying organs or stretching releasing emotions stored in fascia, are not supported by research. Other benefits, such as improved balance, strength and flexibility, and the calming effect of slow breathing for many people, are reasonably well supported. Saying plainly what we know and what we do not protects students and builds your credibility.\n\nFinally, remember your scope. Yoga teachers adapt and refer; they never diagnose. If a student reports sharp pain, pain that persists, numbness or tingling, offer a comfortable alternative and suggest they see a doctor or physical therapist.", "key_points": ["Anatomy gives you precise, shared language for cues", "Bodies vary, so poses should be adapted rather than forced into one shape", "Every lesson follows See it, Feel it, Cue it, Check it", "Separate well supported benefits from popular myths", "Yoga teachers adapt and refer; they never diagnose"], "feel_it": "Stand in Mountain pose with your eyes open. Sway very slowly forward and back, then side to side, for about thirty seconds each. Notice where pressure moves under your feet and which areas of your body start working. Come to stillness and write down three sensations in plain words before you learn any technical terms for them.", "cue_it": ["Let's find a version of this pose that feels steady for you today.", "If anything feels sharp or pinchy, ease out and take Child's pose.", "Notice where the weight sits in your feet before you change anything."]}}, {"id": "1.2", "title": "Anatomical position, planes and axes, seen through three standing poses", "mins": 25, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Anatomical position: the starting map", "points": ["Standing tall, face and feet forward, arms by the sides", "Palms turned forward, thumbs pointing away from the body", "Every movement term is named from this position", "Mountain is anatomical position with palms turned in"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": [], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR", "hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "Turn the palms forward and Mountain becomes anatomical position"}, "notes": "Anatomists needed one agreed starting posture so that words like up, front and inward mean the same thing no matter how a body is lying or twisting. That posture is called anatomical position: standing upright, head and eyes facing forward, feet together or slightly apart and pointing forward, arms hanging by the sides with the palms facing forward. Look at Mountain pose. It is almost identical. The only difference is that in Mountain most students let their palms face their thighs. Ask them to turn the palms forward and they are standing in anatomical position. From now on, every movement term we use is described relative to this map."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Directional terms in pairs", "columns": [{"head": "Up and down, front and back", "points": ["Superior: toward the head", "Inferior: toward the feet", "Anterior: toward the front", "Posterior: toward the back"]}, {"head": "Middle and limbs", "points": ["Medial: toward the midline", "Lateral: away from the midline", "Proximal: closer to the trunk", "Distal: farther from the trunk"]}], "notes": "Directional terms come in opposite pairs, which makes them easier to learn. Superior means toward the head and inferior means toward the feet, so the knee is superior to the ankle. Anterior is the front and posterior is the back, so the kneecap is on the anterior knee. Medial means toward the midline of the body and lateral means away from it, so the big toe is on the medial side of the foot. Proximal and distal are used for limbs: proximal is closer to where the limb attaches to the trunk and distal is farther away. The elbow is proximal to the wrist. You will hear these words throughout this course."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Three planes, three axes", "points": ["Sagittal plane: splits left and right; forward and back movement", "Frontal plane: splits front and back; side to side movement", "Transverse plane: splits top and bottom; turning movement", "Each plane turns around an axis at right angles to it"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m1-planes", "caption": "Each plane has a matching axis of rotation, shown in amber"}, "notes": "A plane is an imaginary flat sheet of glass through the body. The sagittal plane divides the body into left and right parts, and movements in it go forward and back, like folding forward. The frontal plane, also called the coronal plane, divides front from back, and movements in it go out to the side and back in, like lifting your arms in Warrior II. The transverse plane divides top from bottom, and movements in it are turns, like a seated twist. Each movement happens around an axis, a line like a door hinge. The hinge always sits at right angles to the plane of motion. A forward fold swings around a side to side axis through the hips."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Three standing poses, three planes", "columns": [{"head": "Tadasana", "points": ["The neutral reference for all planes", "Close to anatomical position", "Small sways in sagittal and frontal planes"]}, {"head": "Warrior II", "points": ["Arms abducted in the frontal plane", "Front hip externally rotated", "Front knee flexed over the foot"]}, {"head": "Triangle", "points": ["Trunk tilts sideways, frontal plane", "Front hip flexed and externally rotated", "Arms open wide, frontal plane"]}], "notes": "Here is why this module teaches planes through three standing poses. Tadasana, or Mountain, is our neutral reference: it is nearly anatomical position, and the only movement is small sway in the sagittal and frontal planes. Warrior II shows frontal plane work clearly: the arms are lifted out to the sides, which is abduction, and the front thigh is turned out, which is external rotation in the transverse plane. Triangle again opens the arms in the frontal plane and tilts the trunk sideways. Notice that real poses mix planes. The front hip in Triangle is flexed, abducted and externally rotated all at once. Planes are a naming tool, not a rule that poses must stay flat."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: find the planes in Warrior II", "steps": ["From Mountain, lift both arms out to shoulder height: frontal plane", "Step wide, turn the front foot out: rotation in the transverse plane", "Bend the front knee: flexion in the sagittal plane of that leg", "Hold three breaths and name each plane to yourself as you scan"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior2", "highlight": ["armL", "armR", "thighR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR", "hipR", "kneeR"], "caption": "Warrior II uses all three planes at once"}, "notes": "Let's feel the planes rather than just read about them. Start in Mountain and lift both arms out to the sides to shoulder height. That movement happened in the frontal plane. Now step your feet wide and turn your front foot and thigh outward. That turning is rotation in the transverse plane. Now bend the front knee. The knee is bending forward and back relative to that leg, which is sagittal plane motion for the knee. Stay for three breaths and scan: arms, frontal; hip turn, transverse; knee bend, sagittal. You have just found all three planes in one familiar pose. This kind of scan is a habit you will use for every pose."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Cueing Triangle with planes in mind", "cue": "Reach long through your front arm, then tilt sideways from the hips and let both sides of your waist stay long.", "why": "Triangle is mainly hip flexion with trunk tilting in the frontal plane. Keeping both sides of the waist long avoids collapsing into only the lower side.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "triangle", "highlight": ["trunk", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipR"], "caption": "Triangle tilts the trunk mainly in the frontal plane"}, "notes": "Many teachers cue Triangle as if the body is pressed between two panes of glass. It is a useful image for the frontal plane, but take it lightly. Most people's pelvis will naturally turn a little toward the floor, and that is fine; forcing the pelvis flat can strain the knee or lower back. A better emphasis is to reach first and tilt from the hips, keeping both sides of the waist long. Anatomically, the front hip is flexing as the pelvis tips over the front thigh, and the trunk adds some lateral flexion. Give students permission to use a block so the shape serves them, not the other way round."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Lifting the arms out to the sides into Warrior II arms happens mainly in which plane?", "options": ["Sagittal plane", "Frontal plane", "Transverse plane"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Moving the arms out to the side and back in is abduction and adduction in the frontal plane.", "notes": "The frontal plane divides front from back, so any movement out to the side and back in happens there. Sagittal would be lifting the arms forward, as in Warrior I, and transverse would be a turning movement."}], "guide": {"brief": "Anatomical position is the reference posture that every movement term refers to: standing upright, face and feet forward, arms by the sides with palms facing forward. Mountain pose (Tadasana) is almost the same, except that most students rest their palms against their thighs. Because all terms are named from this position, a word like flexion means the same thing whether a student is standing, lying down or upside down.\n\nDirectional terms describe where one structure is relative to another. They come in pairs: superior and inferior (toward the head or feet), anterior and posterior (front or back), medial and lateral (toward or away from the midline) and proximal and distal (closer to or farther from where a limb joins the trunk).\n\nThe body has three cardinal planes. The sagittal plane divides left from right, and movement in it goes forward and back, as in a forward fold. The frontal or coronal plane divides front from back, and movement in it goes out to the side and back, as in lifting the arms in Warrior II. The transverse plane divides top from bottom, and movement in it is rotation, as in a twist. Each movement turns around an axis that sits at right angles to its plane: a side to side axis for sagittal movement, a front to back axis for frontal movement and a vertical axis for transverse movement. Textbooks name these axes in slightly different ways, so focus on the idea of the hinge.\n\nReal poses combine planes. Warrior II lifts the arms in the frontal plane, turns the front thigh out in the transverse plane and bends the front knee. Triangle tilts the trunk mostly in the frontal plane while the front hip flexes. Planes are a naming tool, not a demand that poses stay perfectly flat.", "key_points": ["Anatomical position is Mountain with palms turned forward", "Directional terms come in opposite pairs", "Sagittal: forward and back; frontal: side to side; transverse: turning", "Each plane has an axis at right angles to it, like a hinge", "Most poses combine movements in more than one plane"], "feel_it": "Stand in Mountain and turn your palms forward to find anatomical position. Fold forward a few inches and return, noticing the sagittal plane. Lift your arms out to the sides for the frontal plane, then turn your chest gently left and right for the transverse plane. Finish in Warrior II and name the plane of each joint as you scan from arms to feet.", "cue_it": ["Turn your palms forward and feel your collarbones widen.", "Lift your arms out to the sides, in line with your shoulders.", "Tilt sideways from the hips and keep both sides of your waist long."]}}, {"id": "1.3", "title": "Movement vocabulary: flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation and special terms", "mins": 30, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Movement words come in opposite pairs", "points": ["Flexion bends a joint; extension straightens it", "Abduction moves away from the midline; adduction moves toward it", "Internal rotation turns inward; external rotation turns outward", "Learn one word and you have learned its partner"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m1-movements", "caption": "Paired movement terms, with the first term in red and its partner in green"}, "notes": "Joint movement terms come in pairs of opposites, which halves the work of learning them. Flexion usually decreases the angle between two bones, like bending the knees in Chair, and extension increases it, like straightening back to Mountain. Abduction takes a limb away from the midline, like lifting the arms out into Warrior II, and adduction brings it back. Internal rotation, also called medial rotation, turns the front of a limb toward the midline, and external or lateral rotation turns it away. The front thigh in Warrior II is externally rotated. The diagram also shows some of the special terms we will meet next. Always name the joint and the movement together, such as hip flexion."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Sorting movements by plane", "columns": [{"head": "Sagittal", "points": ["Flexion and extension", "Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion", "Example: Forward Fold"]}, {"head": "Frontal", "points": ["Abduction and adduction", "Lateral flexion of the spine", "Example: Warrior II arms"]}, {"head": "Transverse", "points": ["Internal and external rotation", "Horizontal abduction and adduction", "Example: seated twist"]}], "notes": "Each pair of movements belongs to a plane, and knowing the plane helps you recognise the movement. Flexion and extension happen in the sagittal plane, and so do dorsiflexion and plantarflexion at the ankle. Abduction and adduction, and side bending of the spine, called lateral flexion, happen in the frontal plane. Rotations happen in the transverse plane, and so do horizontal abduction and adduction, where an arm already lifted to shoulder height sweeps back and forth, like opening the arms from in front of you out to Warrior II. One small caution: a few joints, such as the thumb and the shoulder blade, move in ways that do not fit neatly into one plane. The table is a guide, not a rule."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Special terms: ankle, foot and forearm", "points": ["Dorsiflexion lifts the top of the foot toward the shin", "Plantarflexion points the foot, as in tops of feet down", "Inversion turns the sole in; eversion turns it out", "Supination turns the palm up; pronation turns it down"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chair", "highlight": ["shinL", "shinR", "footL", "footR"], "joints": ["ankleL", "ankleR", "kneeL", "kneeR"], "caption": "Chair asks for plenty of ankle dorsiflexion"}, "notes": "Some joints get their own words. At the ankle, dorsiflexion brings the top of the foot toward the shin. Chair pose needs lots of it as the shins lean forward. Plantarflexion points the foot, as when the tops of the feet rest on the mat in Cobra or Child's pose. Inversion tilts the sole of the foot inward and eversion tilts it outward; you feel both as the tiny wobbles of the standing foot in Tree. At the forearm, supination turns the palm up and pronation turns the palm down. Your forearms are pronated in Downward Dog, with palms on the floor. A memory trick: you can hold a bowl of soup in a supinated hand."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Special terms: the shoulder blade", "points": ["Elevation lifts the shoulder blades; depression lowers them", "Protraction wraps them forward around the ribs; retraction draws them back", "Upward rotation turns the socket up as arms go overhead", "Downward rotation returns the blades as the arms lower"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m1-scapula", "caption": "Scapular movements seen from behind"}, "notes": "The shoulder blade, or scapula, slides over the back of the rib cage, and it has its own vocabulary. Elevation is a shrug and depression lowers the blades. Protraction wraps them forward around the ribs, as when you press the floor away in Plank, and retraction draws them toward the spine, as in Cobra. Upward rotation turns the shoulder socket to face upward; it happens automatically whenever you lift your arms overhead, roughly one degree of blade rotation for every two degrees of arm lift. Downward rotation is the return. This matters for cueing. Telling students to pin the shoulders down and back while their arms are overhead fights the upward rotation that the shoulder needs."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Circles, side bends and horizontal sweeps", "points": ["Circumduction: a circle combining flexion, abduction, extension and adduction", "Lateral flexion: bending the spine sideways, as in Gate", "Horizontal abduction: arm at shoulder height sweeps back", "Horizontal adduction: arm at shoulder height sweeps across"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "triangle", "highlight": ["trunk", "armL", "armR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Triangle adds some lateral flexion of the trunk"}, "notes": "A few more terms round out the vocabulary. Circumduction is a circle drawn by a limb, like arm circles or ankle circles in a warm up. It is not a separate kind of movement but a combination of flexion, abduction, extension and adduction in sequence. Lateral flexion means side bending of the spine or neck, as in Gate pose, a standing side bend, or the side tilt in Triangle. Horizontal abduction and adduction describe an arm already lifted to shoulder height. Sweeping it back and out is horizontal abduction, as when the arms open from in front of you into Warrior II. Sweeping it across the chest is horizontal adduction, as in Eagle arms."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: name the joint actions of Warrior II", "steps": ["Front hip: flexed, abducted and externally rotated", "Front knee: flexed toward ninety degrees; front ankle: dorsiflexed", "Back leg: hip abducted, knee extended, foot turned slightly in", "Shoulders: abducted to shoulder height, forearms often pronated"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior2", "highlight": ["thighR", "shinR", "armL", "armR"], "joints": ["hipR", "kneeR", "ankleR", "shoulderL", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Name each joint, then each movement"}, "notes": "Now we practise the most useful skill in this module: describing a pose joint by joint. Come into Warrior II. Start at the front hip: it is flexed because the thigh has moved forward relative to the pelvis, abducted because the leg is away from the midline, and externally rotated because the knee points out over the toes. The front knee is flexed and the front ankle is dorsiflexed as the shin leans forward. The back hip is abducted and the back knee is extended. The shoulders are abducted to about ninety degrees. The forearms are usually pronated, palms down. Say these out loud once, and the pose will never look the same again."}, {"type": "check", "question": "In Chair pose, the shins lean forward over the feet. What is the ankle doing?", "options": ["Plantarflexion", "Inversion", "Dorsiflexion"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The top of the foot and the shin come closer together, which is dorsiflexion.", "notes": "When the shin leans forward over a flat foot, the angle between the top of the foot and the front of the shin gets smaller. That is dorsiflexion. Plantarflexion would be pointing the foot, and inversion would be tilting the sole inward."}], "guide": {"brief": "Movement terms describe what a joint is doing, always relative to anatomical position, and most come in opposite pairs. Flexion usually decreases the angle between two bones, such as bending the knees in Chair, and extension increases it, such as straightening to Mountain. Abduction moves a limb away from the midline and adduction brings it back. Internal (medial) rotation turns the front of a limb toward the midline, and external (lateral) rotation turns it away. Always name the joint with the movement: hip flexion, shoulder abduction, knee extension.\n\nMovements belong to planes. Flexion and extension, and dorsiflexion and plantarflexion, are sagittal. Abduction, adduction and lateral flexion of the spine are frontal. Rotations and horizontal abduction and adduction are transverse. Circumduction combines several movements into a circle, such as arm circles.\n\nSpecial terms apply to particular joints. At the ankle, dorsiflexion brings the top of the foot toward the shin (Chair) and plantarflexion points the foot (tops of feet down in Cobra). Inversion turns the sole inward and eversion turns it outward (the small wobbles of the standing foot in Tree). At the forearm, supination turns the palm up and pronation turns it down (hands on the mat in Downward Dog). The shoulder blade elevates and depresses, protracts and retracts, and rotates upward and downward. Upward rotation is a normal and necessary part of lifting the arms overhead, so cues that pin the shoulders down and back with arms overhead can work against healthy shoulder mechanics.\n\nThe most practical skill is describing a pose joint by joint. In Warrior II the front hip is flexed, abducted and externally rotated, the front knee is flexed, the front ankle is dorsiflexed, the back knee is extended, and the shoulders are abducted. Practise this scan on every pose you teach.", "key_points": ["Name the joint and the movement together, such as hip flexion", "Most movement terms come in opposite pairs", "Sagittal: flexion and extension; frontal: abduction and adduction; transverse: rotation", "Ankle, foot, forearm and shoulder blade each have special terms", "Upward rotation of the shoulder blades is needed for arms overhead", "Describe poses joint by joint, from hips to shoulders"], "feel_it": "Come into Warrior II on your right side. Starting at the front hip and working down to the ankle, say out loud what each joint is doing. Then do the same for the back leg and both shoulders. Change sides and repeat without looking at your notes. Finish by lifting your arms overhead and feeling your shoulder blades rotate upward.", "cue_it": ["Bend your front knee and let the shin lean forward over the ankle.", "Open your arms out to the sides at shoulder height.", "As your arms rise overhead, let your shoulder blades glide up and out."]}}, {"id": "1.4", "title": "Tissues in brief: bone, cartilage, ligament, tendon, fascia, muscle", "mins": 20, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Six tissues you work with in every pose", "points": ["Bone and cartilage form the frame and joint surfaces", "Ligaments join bone to bone; tendons join muscle to bone", "Muscle contracts and fascia wraps and connects", "Blood supply largely sets how fast each tissue adapts"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m1-tissues", "caption": "Six tissues compared by job, blood supply and adaptation"}, "notes": "Every pose loads the same small cast of tissues. Bone forms the rigid frame and the levers that muscles pull on. Cartilage covers the ends of bones inside joints, giving a smooth, low friction surface. Ligaments are tough bands that join bone to bone and help guide joint motion. Tendons join muscle to bone and transmit the pull of the muscle. Muscle is the tissue that contracts. Fascia is the broad name for the connective tissue sheets that wrap and link muscles and organs. One useful pattern: tissues with a rich blood supply, like muscle, adapt and heal fastest, while tissues with a poor supply, like cartilage and ligament, change slowly."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "206", "label": "bones in the adult skeleton", "body": "Bone is living tissue with nerves and blood vessels. It stores minerals, makes blood cells in marrow and remodels in response to load.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["trunk", "thighL", "thighR", "shinL", "shinR"], "joints": [], "caption": "Standing poses load the leg bones and spine"}, "notes": "The adult skeleton has 206 bones. It is easy to think of bone as dry and dead, like a museum skeleton, but living bone has a blood supply and nerves, stores calcium and other minerals, and makes blood cells in its marrow. Bone also remodels throughout life. When it is loaded regularly, as in standing and weight bearing poses, it tends to maintain or gain density; when it is unloaded, as in long bed rest, it loses density. This is one reason weight bearing practice is often recommended for bone health. The effect of yoga alone on bone density is still being studied, so keep claims modest."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Ligament or tendon?", "columns": [{"head": "Ligament", "points": ["Connects bone to bone", "Guides and limits joint motion", "Rich in position sensing nerve endings", "Heals slowly once overstretched"]}, {"head": "Tendon", "points": ["Connects muscle to bone", "Transmits muscle force to bone", "Stiffens and strengthens with loading", "Adapts over months, not days"]}], "notes": "Ligaments and tendons are both made of dense, tightly packed collagen, and students often mix them up. The simple rule is: ligament, bone to bone; tendon, muscle to bone. Ligaments guide and limit joint movement and contain nerve endings that tell the brain where the joint is. They have a limited blood supply, so once overstretched they heal slowly and may stay looser. That is one reason we do not aim to stretch ligaments by hanging into the end range of joints. Tendons carry the force of a muscle to a bone. They respond well to gradual loading but adapt more slowly than muscle, over months rather than days, so progress intense practices gradually."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Fascia: what we know and what we don't", "points": ["Fascia is fibrous connective tissue wrapping muscles and organs", "It is continuous and richly supplied with sensory nerves", "Hands or stretches probably cannot remodel dense fascia quickly", "Claims about releasing stored emotions or toxins lack evidence"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "forwardfold", "highlight": ["trunk", "thighL", "thighR", "shinL", "shinR"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "Forward Fold stretches muscles and their fascial wrappings together"}, "notes": "Fascia has become a hot topic in yoga. Here is what is established. Fascia is fibrous connective tissue that wraps individual muscles, groups of muscles and organs, and it is continuous from region to region. It contains many sensory nerve endings, so it contributes to how we feel stretch and body position. Here is what is not established. Research suggests that dense fascia needs very large forces to deform permanently, so a stretch or a massage probably changes how the nervous system responds rather than remodelling fascia itself in minutes. Claims that fascia stores emotions or toxins that a pose can release have no good evidence. Fascia is interesting enough without the myths."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: tendon, muscle and joint", "steps": ["Sitting, pinch the cord at the back of your heel: your Achilles tendon", "Point and flex the foot; feel the calf muscle bunch and the tendon move", "Come to Downward Dog and pedal the heels slowly", "Notice stretch in the calf belly, not a sharp pull at the tendon or joint"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "dog", "highlight": ["shinL", "shinR", "footL", "footR"], "joints": ["ankleL", "ankleR"], "caption": "Downward Dog stretches the calves and loads the Achilles tendon"}, "notes": "Let's find some of these tissues. Sitting down, pinch the thick cord at the back of your heel. That is the Achilles tendon, the strongest tendon in the body, joining the calf muscles to the heel bone. Point and flex your foot and feel the muscle belly of the calf shorten and lengthen above it. Now come into Downward Dog and pedal your heels slowly. You should feel a broad stretch in the belly of the calf muscle. If a student feels a sharp, localised pull at the tendon itself, or at the back of the knee joint, that is a signal to back off and bend the knees. Muscle stretch is welcome; sharp joint or tendon pain is not."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Which tissue connects muscle to bone?", "options": ["Ligament", "Tendon", "Cartilage"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Tendons connect muscle to bone; ligaments connect bone to bone.", "notes": "Tendon, muscle to bone; ligament, bone to bone. Cartilage covers the joint surfaces of bones and does not connect muscle to anything. If you remember one fact from this lesson, make it this one."}], "guide": {"brief": "Six tissues share the work of every pose. Bone forms the rigid frame and levers of the body. The adult skeleton has 206 bones. Bone is living tissue with a blood supply and nerves; it stores minerals, makes blood cells in its marrow and remodels in response to the loads placed on it.\n\nCartilage covers the ends of bones inside synovial joints, giving a smooth surface that reduces friction. Articular cartilage has no blood vessels and gets nutrients by diffusion from joint fluid, which movement and loading help circulate, so it heals very slowly. Ligaments are dense bands of collagen that join bone to bone, guide and limit joint movement and contain nerve endings that sense joint position. With a limited blood supply, an overstretched ligament heals slowly and may remain loose, which is why yoga aims to stretch muscles rather than hang in the end range of joints. Tendons join muscle to bone and transmit muscle force. They strengthen with gradual loading but adapt more slowly than muscle.\n\nMuscle is the contractile tissue. Skeletal muscle has a rich blood supply and adapts to training within weeks. Fascia is fibrous connective tissue that wraps muscles, groups of muscles and organs, linking them into a continuous web. It is richly supplied with sensory nerves. Research suggests that dense fascia requires very large forces to deform permanently, so stretching and massage probably work mainly through the nervous system rather than by quickly remodelling fascia. Claims that fascia stores emotions or toxins are not supported by evidence.\n\nFor teaching, the practical lesson is about pace and sensation. Muscles respond quickly, tendons and bones more slowly, ligaments and cartilage slowest of all. Encourage a broad stretch in the muscle belly, and treat sharp or joint centred sensations as a signal to ease off.", "key_points": ["Ligament joins bone to bone; tendon joins muscle to bone", "The adult skeleton has 206 living, remodelling bones", "Articular cartilage has no blood vessels and heals very slowly", "Muscle adapts in weeks; tendon and bone take months", "Fascia is real and interesting, but many popular claims are unproven"], "feel_it": "Sit with one leg extended and find your Achilles tendon with your fingers. Point and flex your foot slowly and feel the calf muscle and tendon move. Then come into Downward Dog and pedal your heels for five breaths. Note where you feel stretch and describe whether it is in muscle belly, tendon or joint.", "cue_it": ["Look for a broad stretch in the belly of the muscle, not a sharp pull near the joint.", "Bend your knees as much as you need so the stretch stays in the calves.", "Build intense poses gradually; tendons adapt more slowly than muscles."]}}], "terms": [["Anatomical position", "The reference posture for all movement terms: standing, face and feet forward, palms facing forward. Mountain with palms turned out."], ["Superior and inferior", "Toward the head and toward the feet. In Mountain the knees are superior to the ankles."], ["Medial and lateral", "Toward and away from the midline. The big toe is on the medial side of the foot."], ["Proximal and distal", "Closer to or farther from where a limb joins the trunk. In Downward Dog the wrist is distal to the elbow."], ["Sagittal plane", "The plane dividing left from right; forward and backward movements happen in it, as in a Forward Fold."], ["Frontal (coronal) plane", "The plane dividing front from back; side to side movements happen in it, as when the arms lift into Warrior II."], ["Transverse plane", "The plane dividing top from bottom; rotations happen in it, as in a seated twist."], ["Axis", "The imaginary hinge line a movement turns around, always at right angles to the plane of movement. A forward fold turns around a side to side axis through the hips."], ["Flexion", "A movement that usually decreases the angle between two bones, like bending the knees in Chair."], ["Extension", "A movement that usually increases the angle between two bones, like straightening the knees back to Mountain."], ["Abduction", "Moving a limb away from the midline, like lifting the arms out to the sides in Warrior II."], ["Adduction", "Moving a limb toward the midline, like bringing the arms back down to the sides after Warrior II."], ["Internal and external rotation", "Turning the front of a limb toward (internal) or away from (external) the midline. The front thigh in Warrior II is externally rotated."], ["Lateral flexion", "Bending the spine or neck sideways, as in Gate pose or the side tilt of Triangle."], ["Circumduction", "A circular movement combining flexion, abduction, extension and adduction, like arm circles in a warm up."], ["Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion", "Lifting the top of the foot toward the shin (Chair) and pointing the foot (tops of feet down in Cobra)."], ["Inversion and eversion", "Tilting the sole of the foot inward and outward; you feel both as the standing foot wobbles in Tree."], ["Pronation and supination", "Turning the palm down (hands on the mat in Downward Dog) and turning the palm up (palms up in Savasana)."], ["Protraction and retraction", "Wrapping the shoulder blades forward around the ribs (pressing away in Plank) and drawing them toward the spine (Cobra)."], ["Elevation and depression", "Lifting the shoulder blades toward the ears and lowering them away, as when softening a shrug in Chair."], ["Upward and downward rotation", "Turning the shoulder blade so the socket faces up as the arms rise overhead, and back again as they lower."], ["Horizontal abduction and adduction", "With the arm at shoulder height, sweeping it back and out (opening into Warrior II) or across the chest (Eagle arms)."]], "misconceptions": [["Warrior II and Triangle must be performed flat, as if between two panes of glass.", "The frontal plane is a useful reference, but most pelvises turn slightly toward the floor. Forcing a flat shape can strain knees and the lower back."], ["Shoulders should always be pinned down and back.", "Lifting the arms overhead requires the shoulder blades to rotate upward and elevate a little. Pinning them down fights normal shoulder mechanics."], ["Twists wring toxins out of the organs.", "The liver and kidneys clear waste continuously. There is no evidence that twisting squeezes out toxins, though twists can feel good and improve spinal mobility."], ["Stretching releases emotions stored in fascia.", "Fascia is richly innervated and stretching can feel emotional, but there is no evidence that fascia stores emotions or that poses release them."], ["Ligaments should be stretched to gain flexibility.", "Ligaments stabilise joints and heal slowly. Flexibility work should target muscle, with a broad stretch rather than sharp joint sensation."]], "flashcards": [["Anatomical position", "Standing, face and feet forward, arms by the sides, palms facing forward. Mountain pose with the palms turned out."], ["How does Mountain differ from anatomical position?", "In Mountain the palms usually face the thighs; in anatomical position they face forward."], ["Sagittal plane", "Divides the body into left and right; flexion and extension happen here, as in a Forward Fold."], ["Frontal plane", "Divides the body into front and back; abduction and adduction happen here, as in Warrior II arms."], ["Transverse plane", "Divides the body into top and bottom; rotations happen here, as in a seated twist."], ["Axis for sagittal plane movement", "A side to side axis, like a hinge running through both hips in a Forward Fold."], ["Axis for transverse plane movement", "A vertical axis running head to toe, which the spine turns around in a twist."], ["Flexion", "Usually decreases the angle between bones. The knees and hips flex as you sit into Chair."], ["Extension", "Usually increases the angle between bones. The knees extend as you stand up from Chair."], ["Abduction", "Moving away from the midline. The arms abduct as they lift into Warrior II."], ["Adduction", "Moving toward the midline. The arms adduct as they lower from Warrior II to the sides."], ["External rotation of the hip", "Turning the thigh so the knee points outward, as in the front leg of Warrior II."], ["Internal rotation of the hip", "Turning the thigh so the knee points inward, part of the back leg action in many standing poses."], ["Lateral flexion", "Side bending of the spine or neck, as in Gate pose or the tilt of Triangle."], ["Circumduction", "A circle combining flexion, abduction, extension and adduction, like arm circles in a warm up."], ["Dorsiflexion", "Top of the foot toward the shin, as the shins lean forward in Chair."], ["Plantarflexion", "Pointing the foot, as with the tops of the feet on the mat in Cobra or Child's pose."], ["Inversion and eversion", "Sole tilts in and sole tilts out; the standing foot in Tree uses both to balance."], ["Pronation", "Turning the palm down, as with hands on the mat in Downward Dog."], ["Supination", "Turning the palm up, as with palms up in Savasana."], ["Protraction of the scapula", "Shoulder blades wrap forward around the ribs, as when pressing the floor away in Plank."], ["Upward rotation of the scapula", "The socket turns upward as the arms go overhead, as in Warrior I arms. It is needed for healthy overhead reach."], ["Horizontal abduction", "An arm at shoulder height sweeps back and out, as when the arms open from in front of you into Warrior II."], ["Ligament vs tendon", "Ligament joins bone to bone; tendon joins muscle to bone. The Achilles tendon is loaded in Downward Dog."], ["Why does cartilage heal slowly?", "Articular cartilage has no blood vessels and relies on diffusion from joint fluid, which movement helps circulate."], ["What is fascia?", "Fibrous connective tissue that wraps and links muscles and organs. It is richly innervated, but claims that it stores emotions are unproven."], ["How many bones in the adult skeleton?", "206. Standing poses load them, and bone remodels in response to regular load."], ["Teacher scope", "Adapt and refer, never diagnose. Offer options and suggest a clinician for sharp or persistent pain, numbness or tingling."]], "exercises": [{"type": "mcq", "q": "A student lifts her arms from her sides up to shoulder height in Warrior II. What movement is happening at the shoulders?", "options": ["Flexion", "Abduction", "External rotation", "Elevation"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Lifting the arms out to the sides moves them away from the midline in the frontal plane, which is abduction."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "In Chair pose, the student's shins lean forward over flat feet. What is happening at the ankles?", "options": ["Plantarflexion", "Eversion", "Dorsiflexion", "Inversion"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The top of the foot and the front of the shin move closer together, which is dorsiflexion."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Which plane does a seated twist mainly happen in?", "options": ["Sagittal", "Frontal", "Transverse", "Coronal"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Twisting is rotation, and rotation happens in the transverse plane around a vertical axis."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A student in Warrior II has his front knee pointing out over his second and third toes. What is his front hip doing?", "options": ["Flexion, adduction and internal rotation", "Extension, abduction and external rotation", "Flexion, abduction and external rotation", "Extension only"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The front thigh has moved forward (flexion), away from the midline (abduction) and turned out (external rotation)."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A teacher cues students to pin their shoulders down and back while reaching their arms overhead in Warrior I. What is the problem with this cue?", "options": ["Nothing, it is ideal for every student", "It fights the upward rotation the shoulder blades need for overhead reach", "It causes the hips to internally rotate", "It forces the ankles into plantarflexion"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Arms overhead require the shoulder blades to rotate upward; pinning them down works against that normal motion."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A student asks whether Revolved Triangle will detox her liver. What is the most accurate answer?", "options": ["Yes, twists squeeze toxins out of organs", "No, the liver and kidneys clear waste continuously; twists may still feel good and aid spinal mobility", "Only if held for five minutes", "Yes, but only on an empty stomach"], "answer": 1, "explain": "There is no evidence that twists detoxify organs, but honest benefits such as mobility and enjoyment are fine to mention."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Which tissue has no blood vessels and relies on joint fluid for nutrients?", "options": ["Muscle", "Bone", "Articular cartilage", "Tendon"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Articular cartilage is avascular, which is why it heals so slowly and why movement helps nourish it."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "In Downward Dog a student feels a sharp, pinpoint pull at the back of the heel. What is the best response?", "options": ["Push the heels down harder to stretch the tendon", "Bend the knees and ease off so the stretch moves into the calf muscle", "Tell her she has Achilles tendinitis", "Ignore it, it will pass"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Sharp, localised sensation at a tendon is a signal to back off; teachers adapt and do not diagnose."}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each movement to a pose example", "pairs": [["Dorsiflexion", "Shins lean forward in Chair"], ["Plantarflexion", "Tops of feet on the mat in Cobra"], ["Abduction", "Arms lift out to the sides in Warrior II"], ["Lateral flexion", "Side bend in Gate pose"], ["Pronation", "Palms down on the mat in Downward Dog"], ["Protraction", "Pressing the floor away in Plank"]]}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each plane to its axis", "pairs": [["Sagittal plane", "Side to side axis"], ["Frontal plane", "Front to back axis"], ["Transverse plane", "Vertical, head to toe axis"], ["Axis in general", "The hinge line a movement turns around"]]}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each tissue to its main job", "pairs": [["Ligament", "Joins bone to bone"], ["Tendon", "Joins muscle to bone"], ["Cartilage", "Smooth covering of joint surfaces"], ["Muscle", "Contracts to produce movement"], ["Fascia", "Wraps and links muscles and organs"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each movement into its main plane", "buckets": ["Sagittal", "Frontal", "Transverse"], "items": [["Hip flexion in a Forward Fold", "Sagittal"], ["Knee extension standing up from Chair", "Sagittal"], ["Ankle dorsiflexion in Chair", "Sagittal"], ["Arms lifting into Warrior II", "Frontal"], ["Side bend in Gate pose", "Frontal"], ["Stepping feet wide for Triangle", "Frontal"], ["Seated spinal twist", "Transverse"], ["Front thigh turning out in Warrior II", "Transverse"], ["Arms sweeping from front to sides at shoulder height", "Transverse"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each claim: well supported or not supported by evidence", "buckets": ["Well supported", "Not supported"], "items": [["Regular practice can improve balance", "Well supported"], ["Weight bearing loads stimulate bone remodelling", "Well supported"], ["Slow breathing can calm many people", "Well supported"], ["Twists wring toxins from the liver", "Not supported"], ["Fascia stores emotions released by stretching", "Not supported"], ["Everyone should achieve the same alignment in Triangle", "Not supported"], ["Muscle adapts to training faster than tendon", "Well supported"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each special term by the body part it describes", "buckets": ["Ankle and foot", "Forearm", "Shoulder blade"], "items": [["Dorsiflexion", "Ankle and foot"], ["Inversion", "Ankle and foot"], ["Eversion", "Ankle and foot"], ["Pronation", "Forearm"], ["Supination", "Forearm"], ["Protraction", "Shoulder blade"], ["Upward rotation", "Shoulder blade"], ["Depression", "Shoulder blade"]]}, {"type": "order", "title": "Put the steps for naming a joint action in order", "items": ["Picture the body in anatomical position", "Choose one joint to look at", "Identify which plane the bone moved in", "Name the movement with its joint, such as hip flexion"]}, {"type": "order", "title": "Order these tissues from richest blood supply to poorest", "items": ["Skeletal muscle", "Bone", "Tendon", "Articular cartilage"]}], "self_check": [["What is anatomical position and why do we need it?", "Standing upright with face and feet forward and palms facing forward. It gives a single reference so every movement term means the same thing in any posture."], ["Name the three cardinal planes and one pose movement in each.", "Sagittal: hip flexion in a Forward Fold. Frontal: arm abduction into Warrior II. Transverse: spinal rotation in a seated twist."], ["What is an axis, and how does it relate to its plane?", "An axis is the hinge line a movement turns around. It always lies at right angles to the plane in which the movement happens."], ["Describe the front hip in Warrior II using three movement terms.", "The front hip is flexed, abducted and externally rotated."], ["Why can the cue to pin the shoulders down and back be unhelpful when the arms are overhead?", "Overhead reach needs the shoulder blades to rotate upward and elevate slightly. Pinning them down works against that normal movement."], ["What is the difference between a ligament and a tendon?", "A ligament joins bone to bone and guides joint motion; a tendon joins muscle to bone and transmits muscle force."], ["What can you honestly say about fascia to a curious student?", "Fascia is connective tissue that wraps and links muscles and organs and is rich in sensory nerves. Claims that it stores emotions or can be quickly remodelled by stretching are not supported by good evidence."], ["A student reports tingling down the leg in a Forward Fold. What do you do?", "Have them ease out of the pose and offer a comfortable alternative. Suggest they see a doctor or physical therapist, without guessing at a diagnosis."]]};
